\section{Route a Handler Result Without Expanding the Handler's Scope}

Giving a computation separate success and failure continuations makes explicit where exception handling ends. For an illustrative strict core, consider \verb|finish(try fetch() catch e -> recover(e))| with ordinary synchronous exceptions. Assume the handler catches failures delivered by \texttt{fetch}, not failures from its own body or from the enclosing call to \texttt{finish}. The input to the lowering is the expression and a pair of outer continuations \(k,h\) for normal and exceptional returns. The output is a CPS term that evaluates the same call sequence and delivers its final outcome to the correct outer continuation. Here the continuations are local, nonescaping control destinations.

\begin{verbatim}
lower(fetch, recover, finish, k, h):
  join after_try(v):
    # The enclosing consumer is outside the handler.
    tailcall finish(v, success=k, failure=h)
  join caught(e):
    # A new failure in recovery goes to the outer handler.
    tailcall recover(e, success=after_try, failure=h)
  tailcall fetch(success=after_try, failure=caught)
test driver:
  k(value) = record Return(value)
  h(error) = record Raise(error)
\end{verbatim}

Suppose \texttt{fetch} emits \texttt{read} and throws \texttt{Missing}. Recovery emits \texttt{default} and returns \(4\). If \texttt{finish} emits \texttt{format} and multiplies by ten, the resulting log is \verb|[read, default, format]| and the outcome is \texttt{Return(40)}. If \texttt{finish} instead throws \texttt{BadFormat}, the same three events occur but the outcome is \texttt{Raise(BadFormat)}. Passing \texttt{caught} to \texttt{finish} would handle \texttt{BadFormat} as though it came from the protected read, and might repeat recovery indefinitely.

The invariant is that exactly one dynamic operation has \texttt{caught} as a failure destination: the protected call. Both normal paths meet at \texttt{after\_try} before the consumer, and a failure in recovery skips that join altogether. This boundary remains significant if an optimizer inlines all three operations; the relevant question is not where the text ended up, but which exceptional edge each operation now takes.
